Dr. Mey Kalyan: STEM Education Strategy and Industry 4.0 will make Cambodia a high middle income country
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Mey Kanyan, Chairman of the Board of the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP), on October 14, 2021, emphasized that STEM Education Strategy and Industrial Development Research 4.0 will promote Cambodia to become a middle-income country. Advanced.
Dr. Mey Kanyan made this remark during a press conference on the establishment of the Cambodia-Japan Digital Production Center (CJDM) at the Royal University of Phnom Penh on October 14, 2021.
Dr. Mey Kanyan stated that Cambodia will achieve its long-term goal of becoming a high middle-income country and a developed country after a number of policies, strategies, development and reforms.
Policies, strategies, development and reforms include: industrial development plans, economic diversification plans, STEM education strategies, and the promotion of research, development, and industrial innovation 4.0. This is the additional confirmation of Dr. Mey Kanyan.
Dr. Chet Jelly, Rector of the Royal University of Phnom Penh, said that RUPP and the Japanese-German joint venture and the world's number one high-tech machinery manufacturer have collaborated to produce high-capacity resources and training programs in the field. Digital.
Dr. Chet Jelly added that RUPP University has adopted the Japanese model of innovation, which promotes economic and social development, as it supports a complete production chain, from knowledge to the final product.
Dr. Nguon Phorn Pheakdey, Dean of the Faculty of Engineering, RUPP, said that the Royal University of Phnom Penh provides opportunities for all Cambodian students and he expects graduates to work for high-tech companies in the short term.
Dr. Nguon Phorn Pheakdey continued that in the future, they will play an important role in supporting other industries in the country to move towards a new revolutionary smart digital production, thus improving the Cambodian manufacturing sector. Ms. Youk Sambath: The Royal Government is planning to open economic activities and receive foreign tourists again (Video inside)
