ASEAN to review trade competition rules as e-commerce boosts impact SME (Video inside) (Bangkok): ASEAN member states plan to review trade competition regulations as they rise Concerned about the rapidly changing business infrastructure fueled by the Kovid-19 pandemic. According to a report by the Bangkok Post.
Sakon, who attended the first meeting of the ASEAN Business Competitiveness Bureau chief via video conference this week, said SMEs in ASEAN were being affected by the growth of online platforms and e-commerce. He added that another concern was the increase in mergers and acquisitions (M&A) by large and wealthy companies in the region.
In 2007, the ASEAN Economic Ministers approved the establishment of an ASEAN Expert Group on Competitiveness to be the official authority for the Association, which includes representatives from all member states' trade competition offices. .
Sakon told the Bangkok Post that the first meeting agreed to raise a handbook to manage the digital economy, as fruit online has had a significant impact on many local businesses in Asean. The deadline for completing this manual is next year.
The Bangkok Post also reported that ASEAN plans to submit a trade competition manual to the ASEAN Summit next year.
Mr Sakon added that ASEAN members needed their own guidelines to govern M&A due to the growing M&A vision in the ASEAN region, and countries also needed to build their own capacity to streamline the implementation of trade competition law.
