In its latest World Economic Outlook report, the Washington-based fund expects the global economy to grow 5.9 percent this year, down 0.1 percentage points from last month's forecast. July will rise from a 3.1 percent decline in 2020. The IMF kept its forecast for 2022 unchanged at 4.9 percent.
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) has cited escalating threats to economic growth due to 19 types of Kovid virus, supply disruptions, rising inflation and rising food and fuel costs, Bloomberg reported. The IMF has also lowered figures for some countries, especially low-income ones, where access to vaccines remains limited.
Among the world's largest economies, the IMF cut its forecast for the US economy by one percentage point to 6 percent due to supply shortages, but raised its forecast for 2022 from four percent, Bloomberg reported. .9 percent to 5.2 percent.
The fund has raised its forecast for the eurozone to 5 percent this year from 4.6 percent and kept its forecast for 2022 at 4.3 percent.
Growth forecasts for Japan, Britain, Germany and Canada have been cut for this year but raised for 2022. The low-income economy is expected to grow just 3 percent this year, with the IMF down 0.9 percentage points from its July forecast.
