By 2018, the ADB is committed to securing at least 75% of its total operations to support climate-related activities, and its climate financial resources will reach at least $ 80 billion by 2030. Today's announcement raises the ambition of these financing.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) expects its total climate financing for 2019-2021 to reach about $ 17 billion.
The increased financial ambitions are an important part of ADB's efforts to support its member developing countries (DMCs). In the face of the intermittent problems of the Kovid 19 pandemic and the climate crisis, many DMCs are taking drastic measures to promote a green, resilient and environmentally friendly recovery.
The additional $ 20 billion will support the climate agenda in five key areas:
* First, new ways to reduce climate change include energy conservation, energy efficiency and low carbon transportation. The ADB expects its total climate change mitigation funding to reach $ 66 billion.
* Second, the project expands adaptation to change. Projects in these climate-sensitive sectors, including urban services, agriculture and water, will be designed primarily for effective climate adaptation and resilience. ADB expects its total financing for adaptation activities to reach $ 34 billion.
Fifth, support for reform in the DMCs to implement policy-based lending activities, support policies and institutions for strengthening climate resilience and climate change mitigation.
The Secretary-General also stressed that as COP26 draws closer, he remains deeply concerned about the lack of progress on these priorities. Guterres said the political package to be presented at the Glasgow conference needed at least three key elements: reducing emissions, financing climate action to developing countries and adapting to climate change. .
As the COVID-19 pandemic and climate change pose new and unique challenges for low- and middle-income countries, the UN Secretary-General called on the finance ministers to review the level. Of receiving official development assistance to improve access to finance for those countries.
