On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Senior Minister Ly Thuch praised the historic relationship between Cambodia and Hungary and considered Hungary a good friend and close cooperator with Cambodia.
On that occasion, on behalf of Samdech Techo Prime Minister and Chairman of the Mine Authority, Senior Minister Ly Thuch thanked the generous government and people of Hungary for providing grant funding to assist the mine action sector in Cambodia through the Ministry. Cambodia Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation at US $ 28,570.
The Senior Minister informed the Chargé d'Affaires that the Mine Authority has used the grant funding to implement two projects, including: First, a demining project in Damnak Chang Eur village, Prey Thom commune, Kep district, covering an area of 46,009 Square meters and the second: the project to verify the area of mine land in the actual land in Kep and Koh Kong provinces.
The Senior Minister continued that the Mine Authority received permission from Samdech Techo Prime Minister on September 6, 2021 to select Kep Province as the first province to end the mine threat with the least impact to implement the first province. Threatened with the support of the Hungarian government and the selection of the national operator CMAC as a implementer in providing safe land to the people of Kep province.
At the same time, the Senior Minister confirmed that on October 1, 2021, he led the technical team to inspect the demining activities of the national operator CMAC on the Hungarian grant project, which resulted in the discovery of 5 mines. Dozens of war explosives and thousands of fragments.
He added that after the demining work in Damnak Chang Eur village, the Mine Authority will report to the Government of Hungary through the Chargé d'Affaires of the Hungarian Embassy in Cambodia on the results of the grant and plans to hold a village declaration ceremony. Damnak Chang Eur is a village that is no longer threatened by landmines.
At the same time, on behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the Senior Minister requested the Government of Hungary to continue to support the implementation of mine action village mechanisms in two other villages in Damnak Chang Eur District, Kep Province, including Chamkar Bei Village. And Chamkar Chek village, which has 10 mine-affected plantations on 625,319 square meters of land, to make Kep the first mine-free province in Cambodia.
Mr. Chargé d'Affaires expressed his pleasure at being involved with the Royal Government of Cambodia for the first time in mine action in Cambodia, and stated that this was the first meeting between him, a representative of the Hungarian government and the leaders of the Mine Authority, and that he planned to To visit Kep province to check the results of demining.
