Mr. Pen Bona, on behalf of the media, expressed his interest: "I am interested in the issues that have arisen in recent days in connection with the misrepresentation of The Guardian in the United Kingdom:
First, misrepresentation is a big issue that journalists or the media must be extremely careful. This is why journalists need to make verification the top priority to avoid misrepresentation, because misrepresentation can have serious consequences, especially if it affects the reputation of others.
Second, however, unintentional mistakes often occur (excluding intentional mistakes). The important thing is to acknowledge the mistake and correct it. This is the responsibility of journalists as well as media institutions.
Thirdly, the reporters who misrepresented the news must also correct it. Adjustments must be made in accordance with the size of the information it publishes. If only the information quoted from the original content is reproduced by the original author, the reproducer must also be re-edited to the full text of the original author.
Fourthly, if you quote the information and use it to expand the meaning beyond the information that the original author wrote, the correction must also match the meaning that it publishes. In other words, the size of the broadcast, the correction must be the same because it is to erase the error of the information it publishes.
Fifth, the correction of information must not imply any mockery.
