Nak Phea, president of Meanchey Snuol Tret community, said the community began exporting dried ivory, ivory leaves to Thailand last month and so far has only exported about 10 tonnes. Apart from the small amount of cultivation, farmers are now facing production due to the rainy season, no day for drying.
The community leader confirmed that the Meanchey Snuol Tret community has more than 100 member farmers growing ivory leaves on more than 200 hectares. In the future, the community plans to expand the cultivation to 1,000 hectares for the production of lemongrass leaves for export.
Mr. Nak Phea said that the price of dried lemongrass leaves that the community buys from farmers is 1,400 riel per kilogram. However, the community buys lemongrass leaves grown according to organic standards without the use of chemicals.
He said that in addition to the Thai market, he was looking at the Chinese market, which is a major market for direct exports.
As for fresh lemongrass root, it can not be exported abroad, only the local market, the community can supply about 20 to 50 tons per day.
He added that in addition to exporting dried lemongrass, he was also looking for markets to export lemongrass to other countries, such as Singapore and Korea.
