The ADB has stated that the grant includes a $ 25 million loan as additional funding for the Greater Mekong Subregion (GMS) Health Security Project. It will promote laboratory services and infection control monitoring at 81 provincial and district referral hospitals across the country. It will improve disease surveillance and response systems, control of 19 Kovid outbreaks and follow-up sources at provincial and district central health agencies.
Cambodia will be able to control 19 cases of smallpox by 2020, but the situation has worsened since February 2021.
Given the threat posed by the new Kovid 19 mutation, which can spread rapidly, support for this project to strengthen surveillance, response, outbreaks and health services is timely. The project will address social issues caused by epidemics, such as training hospital staff to identify and support citizens affected by gender-based violence and mental health issues.
This project implements the Asian Development Bank's Country Partnership Strategy 2019-2023 for Cambodia, which prioritizes human resource strengthening. It will contribute to the common goal of the GMS countries to improve their response to serious public health threats.
