General Knowledge: Someone arrived in the Americas 400 years before Christopher Columbus
With the discovery of a new territory, North America, Ericsson proved to the other Vikings that there was indeed a resource-rich territory on the other side of the ocean. There Ericsson saw all kinds of plants, and there was an abundance of trees, fish, and wildlife.
However, Ericsson did not spend much time in North America, he returned to Greenland and eventually became a leader there in place of his father. Ericsson died between 1019 and 1025 CE His greatest legacy is to pave the way for the next generation of Greenlands to follow in his footsteps in creating new villages in North America where he first discovered them.
It is claimed that Christopher Columbus first heard of the new land when he first visited Norway in 1477. Although Ericsson's discovery was well-known in many European ports, no other Europeans arrived in North America after Leaf Ericsson until Christopher Columbus passed away. To the archipelago of the Caribbean Sea as well as South America from 1492 AD.
Although Christopher Columbus reached the Caribbean archipelago as well as South America, he did not reach the United States today. However, the world considers Christopher Columbus to be the first to reach this vast continent.
