Nearly 300 Cambodian Blue Hat soldiers repatriated to Cambodia after humanitarian mission in Mali
(Phnom Penh): The Airport Engineering Force No. 972 and the Explosive Ordnance Disposal Force (EOD) No. 292, 298 people, including 25 women, the 7th Cambodian Army in Mali, returned home in the morning. On October 4, 2021, after successfully completing the humanitarian mission to keep the peace under the United Nations umbrella.
The reception ceremony for the Airport Engineering Force No. 972 and the Explosive Ordnance Disposal Force (EOD) No. 882 was held in the presence of General Sem Sovanny, Vice Chairman of the National Committee and Director General of the National Center for Peacekeeping Forces Management, Demining and Remnants of War. As well as some Blue Hat Army leaders at the VIP Airport.
In addition, upon returning to the airport, all forces were immediately sampled and sent to the Ministry of Health by the Ministry of Health for 21 days at the Kampong Speu Multi-National Peacekeeping Forces Training School and required to be trained at home. Individuals for an additional 14 days to ensure that no Blue Hat troops are positive for Kovid 19 after completing the mission for the motherland.
After 14 days, all forces will be tested twice, confirming that the results are negative, all forces will be tested for malaria before being allowed to leave the law in their respective homes.
It should be noted that since 2006 until now, Cambodia has sent more than 7,000 peacekeepers to carry out humanitarian missions with the United Nations in nine countries, including: Sudan, South Sudan, Chad, Lebanon, Syria, Mali, Central Africa, Cyprus and Yemen.
Currently, Cambodia has nearly 800 Blue Hat troops, including nearly 80 women, who are carrying out peacekeeping missions under the United Nations umbrella in Lebanon, Sudan, South Sudan, Mali and the Central African Republic.
