This meeting was held on October 1, 2021 with the participation of representatives from the National Committee for Road Safety, Ministry of Public Works and Transport, Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, National Social Security Fund, Ministry of Women's Affairs. , Ministry of Economy and Finance, General Commission of National Police and sub-national government institutions, industry stakeholders and civil society organizations.
Mr. Kim Panha, Country Director of the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation (AIP Foundation), said that the consultation meeting encouraged the participation of stakeholders and traffic safety leaders to provide experience, advice and practical solutions. The results of the study are an important factor in protecting the lives of factory workers who travel on the streets every day.
William Conklin, Solidarity Center Country Director for Cambodia, emphasized that the meeting was important because it brought a multidisciplinary experience and interest in solving systemic transportation problems, including the development of safer vehicles. Acne affects many Cambodian workers.
The Asian Injury Prevention Foundation and VF Corporation will use the results of the research and solutions as well as the improvements gained from the consultation meeting to implement strategies and actions going forward.
The research aims to examine and identify best practices and practices to transition from unsafe joint transport to safer means, which in turn will save lives.
