China raises 20% rare earths quota to stabilize production supply chain
China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology and Ministry of Natural Resources have announced that China will now produce 168,000 tons of rare earths, the newspaper reported. In February this year, Chinese officials announced a quota of 84,000 tons of rare earths for the first half of this year, and the same amount will be maintained in the second half.
The quota increase will be for the fourth year in a row, currently rising to an all-time high.
Rare ore is an important raw material for use in the technology industry, ranging from the use in electric motors to smartphones, televisions and satellites. China is a source of 60% of the world's rare earths. Rare minerals can also be used in military drones and missiles.
Japanese producers who need rare earths for their production lines closely monitor Chinese production quotas every year.
In January this year, the Chinese government announced a draft law designed to tighten controls on the rare earths industry. The law and the increase in mineral output rarely go against US pressure.
