(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) has announced that the owners of overweight trucks that the Ministry has detained will accept their vehicles from the date of this notice until October 18, 2021.
The announcement was made after the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, allowed the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to return the vehicles to their owners. Over 20% of the overweight vehicles seized by the ministry were 229 vehicles.
With the decision to return the car to its original owner, the following four conditions must be met:
1: The owner of the company and the owner of the overweight vehicle that has been detained and has already paid the fine can pick up the vehicle at the vehicle storage location from the date of this notice until October 18, 2021 and make a contract to stop committing transportation offenses for the second time.
2: Separately, the owner of the company and the owner of the overweight vehicle that has been detained and has not yet paid the legal fine must come to settle and pay the fine before October 18, 2021 as a case in point. During this period, the Steering Committee for the Inspection of Overweight Vehicles will take action in accordance with the procedures and legal provisions in force.
3. The Steering Committee for the Inspection of Overweight Trucks will strive to disseminate more information about laws and regulations to the general public to better understand and understand, including efforts to continue to enforce the law. Without understanding or exempting a second time for any vehicle that transports overweight.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said this on the morning of October 1, 2021 at the Ministry of Public Works and Transport at a press conference on the release of 229 overweight vehicles ahead of schedule after the decision of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said, "Samdech has recommended that we make further efforts to enforce this law. This is the first and last time we have allowed the release of a car seized during the year. "Our traditional Khmer Pchum Ben festival and the revitalization of our economy by bringing that car into business to support the growth of our national economy, but not a second time."
Mr. Sun Chanthol also confirmed that the release will start from now until October 18, only for vehicles that have already paid the fine and vehicles that have not paid the fine must be paid in advance. On the 18th, if not, the car will continue to be detained, not released earlier.
At the same time, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol also instructed the Ministry of Public Works and the inspection team to inspect all overweight vehicles to continue to tighten the law even more after the release of the truck. The 229 vehicles will continue to advise drivers to stop overweight to participate in road maintenance.
Please note that in Article 60. Users of heavy transport vehicles transporting goods on the road:
First, not weighing at the weighing station on the road network shall be fined 500,000 Riels.
