* Summary of facts and developments:
Upon departure, the MT Strovolos shut down its automatic identification system, making it impossible to locate the ship, which could endanger human life at sea. Despite requests to return, the ship refused to return to Cambodian waters. A number of allegations have been made in Cambodia against the crew, including allegations of crude oil theft.
Exercising its rights and obligations in accordance with national and international law, the Royal Government of Cambodia requested and contacted the Indonesian authorities and through the Interpol to arrest and extradite the crew to the Kingdom of Cambodia in accordance with the procedures. As defined in the ASEAN Treaty on Mutual Legal Assistance in the field of criminal and related Cambodian law.
Extradition and mutual legal assistance are common around the world to prevent and curb criminal offenses.
The Ministry of Mines and Energy knows that the crew of the MT Strovolos were previously detained in the Republic of Indonesia during July 2021 because they entered Indonesian waters without proper permission and due to the shutdown. Automatic identification of the ship. The captain has also been tried and convicted of the crime and is currently serving a prison sentence and is being fined.
At all stages, the Royal Government of Cambodia always respects and continues to respect the rights of all crew members. But their crimes must be punished according to the law. Enforcing such a law is not immoral.
The Royal Government of Cambodia is seeking justice for those charged with serious crimes after the completion of legal proceedings in Indonesia. The right to a fair and expedited trial is guaranteed to all individuals extradited to Cambodia to be tried for violations of the law, as in this case.
* Correction of false information:
When the MT Strovolos left Cambodian waters illegally, it was being leased by Chris Energy, a company contracted to develop the Cambodian oil field. Disputes between shipowners and ship managers and Chris Energy regarding ship leases may have arisen, but the issue is not relevant to the Kingdom of Cambodia.
In addition, they should now arrange for the return of crude oil to the legal owner, the Kingdom of Cambodia, and should abandon any attempt to demand money from the Kingdom of Cambodia for their dispute with Chris. Energy.
