Commentary: Will the United States, which lacks honesty and virtue, gain the trust of the people of the world?
In the United States, the American credit culture is involved in almost every activity in an individual's daily life. When looking for a job, the enterprise will check the private credit report until the time of the relationship, Americans will also check the credit records of the parties. Therefore, it is not difficult for individuals or enterprises with credit problems to do business in society. Credit seems to be a private asset, the concept is widely accepted by Americans.
But in dealing with international relations, the United States has always acted virtuously and has become a bad example of a lack of virtue on the international stage. Faced with debate over climate change, the United States staged a "join, withdraw and re-join" scene of the Paris Agreement.
In 2019, to curb China's development, the US government decided to raise tariffs on Chinese products exported to the United States, which provoked a trade war on China, which had a negative impact on global economic development.
In June 2019 in Osaka, Japan, the United States promised "no more tariffs on Chinese products", but then on August 1, the United States announced another tariff increase on Chinese products. The sudden move by the United States has hit international markets hard and sent global stock markets plummeting.
On September 21, 2021, US President Joe Biden addressed the United Nations General Assembly for the first time since taking office, stating that the United States does not want a "new Cold War." No, and be prepared to cooperate with any country to meet the challenges.
But just days before Joe Biden's speech at the UN General Assembly, the United States betrayed France, the "longest-standing ally", and the United States hijacked an agreement between Australia and France on submarine trade. It is valued at about $ 36,500,000 and has formed a new security alliance with Australia and the United Kingdom called AUKUS.
