Cambodia's economy grew at an average annual rate of 7.8% from 1998 to 2019, but contracted by 3.1% in 2020 due to the 19-year epidemic. The country's transport sector faces challenges such as inadequate infrastructure investment and sustainability issues. Most of Cambodia's rural and provincial roads are unpaved and the capacity of national roads is limited.
To address these challenges, the Royal Government has developed a national plan to build high-quality, sustainable and climate-resilient infrastructure that can meet population needs and job growth during 2030. Road construction in the new project is included in the list of national plans.
The first phase of the ADB-approved project in 2017 covers 147 km of national roads in Prey Veng, Siem Reap and Svay Rieng provinces along the Southern Greater Mekong Subregion Economic Corridor.
In the second phase, the improvement of national roads will demonstrate cost-effectiveness, sustainability and environmentally friendly biotechnology, including the protection of dams and slopes. Improving provincial roads will use environmentally friendly liquid soils to help expand the use of locally available raw materials.
The project also aims to help the Ministry of Public Works and Transport improve road management and develop sustainable road maintenance methods using effective road maintenance contracts. It will also raise awareness of road safety and health in the communities where the project is being implemented.
The road project follows the ADB Country Partnership Strategy for Cambodia, which aims to help the country improve its competitiveness and economic diversification. This project is also a continuation of the ADB Road and Rail Program in Cambodia.
