New markets in the Middle East and North Africa pave the way for Cambodian processing companies to export agricultural products to the market and get to know more international markets, as well as bring Cambodian traders closer to foreign buyers. More.
"Launching a digital export mission to the Middle East and North Africa is a good sign of the partnership between the private and public sectors," said Seang Thai, Secretary of State at the Ministry of Commerce. "Diversify Cambodian markets and products to target markets that contribute to bringing foreign currency into the national economy."
He added that this activity is in line with the Ministry of Commerce's action plan to promote and promote the sale of Cambodian products abroad.
Mr. Oknha Hun Lak, CEO of RICHFARM ASIA, said that this digital pepper marketing program has helped potential Cambodian products, both fresh and processed products, as well as other spices, to the international stage. Through face-to-face meetings with sellers and buyers online that we have never had before, this is the first step in launching a potential product in Cambodia to the Middle East and North Africa markets.
He added that the exhibition is a direct promotion of Cambodian products to the Middle East and North Africa markets, called online technology trading, without having to send products directly.
He added: "I think this program is new and the best in my company because it is fast, easy for both sellers and buyers, which allows sellers - buyers to communicate online. In addition, it can show the history of the company, other products, as well as prices between each other.
Mr. Seang Thai added that in order to succeed in this mission and to maintain business partners and markets, enterprises and companies participating in this program must adhere to four main principles: goods must be: 1: High quality. 2: Safety and hygiene standards. 3: Aesthetic packaging. 4: Reasonable price, competitive and honest, strive to supply goods on time.
