The Thai Emergency Management Agency (DDPM) said on Tuesday that 30 provinces in the central ASEAN country were hit by the tropical storm, which caused heavy rains and landslides. More than 70,000 people were drowned.
According to the DDPM, the flood situation in seven provinces has eased slightly, while 23 others continue to be severe. However, Thai authorities have already predicted that more heavy rains will occur in the northeast and central parts of the country in the next three days.
On the morning of September 28, 2021, Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice-Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management and Samdech Techo Hun Sen's Personal Staff on Disaster Management, led a joint rescue force to the flooded area and assisted. Evacuate the flooded people in Mongkul Borei district to a safe place in Bot Trang commune, Mongkul Borei district.
It should be noted that so far, many rescue forces, including the National Disaster Management Committee, the Army from the 5th and 4th Military Divisions, the police force and other relevant forces have been deployed in Banteay Meanchey province. They are ready to help people from the floods.
Please note that as of the night of September 27, 2021, floods, rains and waterfalls from Thailand affected 6 cities, districts equal to 30 communes. Affected 8,511 families and evacuated 930 families.
