According to Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, all of them have been handed over to the Cambodian authorities for processing. International analysis: Will the AUKUS alliance push the Indo-Pacific region into a nuclear arms race?
BEIJING (Reuters) - A pledge to help Australia build a nuclear-powered submarine under the AUKUS tripartite military alliance between the United States, Britain and Australia has not only angered France but also raised concerns. From the possibility of a new Cold War eruption and a nuclear arms race in the Indo-Pacific region.
Under the AUKUS alliance, the United States and Britain have agreed to transfer nuclear-powered submarine technology to Australia, becoming the third country to receive such technology from the United States after Britain. Nuclear submarines will help strengthen the Australian Navy, enabling them to operate more effectively in the Indo-Pacific region, as well as joining the United States in curbing growing Chinese influence in the region.
Although they know that China, the United States, Britain and France have never cared about the treaty and have always sent warships across the South China Sea, ASEAN does not want to see military action or tensions. It erupts into regional conflict and spreads to its borders or countries. On the other hand, in principle, ASEAN prefers to take advantage of neutral politics without choosing any superpower.
But many experts believe that the growing rivalry between the United States and China will make it harder for ASEAN to pursue a policy of neutrality in a context where US President Joe Biden's Indo-Pacific strategy appears to be a clear signal. Up to the countries that want to stand on the side of the United States or on the side of China.
Source: SCMP (Monday, September 27, 2021) The Seventh Committee of the National Assembly inspected the teaching and learning situation in public and private educational institutions in Kampong Speu province.
(Kampong Speu): On the morning of September 27, 2021, a delegation of the National Assembly Committee on Education, Youth, Sports, Cult, Religious Affairs, Culture and Tourism led by Mr. HUN Many, Chairman of the Commission, went to Kampong Speu Province to inspect the teaching situation. And study in colleges at public and private educational institutions in the province.
Mr. Vy Samnang, Governor of Kampong Speu Province, on behalf of the administration, civil servants and teachers throughout Kampong Speu Province, would like to express his proud welcome that the Seventh Committee of the National Assembly has selected Kampong Speu Province as the first province to review the teaching and learning situation. In collections at public and private institutions.
As a Member of Parliament and Chairman of the Seventh Commission, Mr. Hun Many has always been concerned about the well-being of the people, especially the young people, the students, who are the future pillars of the country.
The presence of the parliamentary delegation on this occasion showed the attention to monitor the implementation of the duties of the Royal Government at all levels, which is an important role of the legislature.
According to the report of Mr. Thorn Tharin, Director of the Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports, after the Royal Government decided to allow the establishment of educational institutions for students to study in Kampong Speu, there are public and private educational institutions, a total of 874 (equivalent to 83.55%) on a total of 1,046 educational institutions throughout the province (as of September 22, 2021).
The schools that have not yet opened are all located in high-risk urban areas and where there are still explosions in the community. Overall, the process of reopening schools in Kampong Speu has been smooth and has received applause and support from students as well as parents and there have been no cases of infection in schools so far.
In the meeting, Mr. HUN Many, Chairman of the Seventh Committee, on behalf of the leadership of the National Assembly, expressed his appreciation and appreciation to the Board of Governors, the leadership of the Department of Education, Youth and Sports, especially teachers who have worked hard to protect the age. People's lives are safe, especially to continue teaching students, even in difficult circumstances.
Mr. Hun Many said that the recent opening of a number of schools across the country is a new positive sign for Cambodian society that occurs because:
* Implement the correct policy of the Royal Government, especially the implementation of the national vaccination campaign for people across the country to build immunity in the community, as of September 24, 2021, the population from 6 years old. A total of 12,950,149 (equivalent to 80.94% of the total population of 16 million) received at least one dose of Kovid 19 vaccine.
After the meeting, the delegation continued to visit two educational institutions located in Kampong Speu province to directly inspect the teaching process in those schools, including Santepheap Primary School and Sokha Phally High School.
On that occasion, the Chairman of the Commission provided some materials to the school, including: masks, hand sanitizers, mobile labs and funds to teachers.
Please note that the Seventh Commission has been closely monitoring the status of the process of opening schools across the country.
