People with disabilities urge the Royal Government to help reopen the massage business
(Phnom Penh): While many service businesses in the tourism sector have been suspended due to the Kovid-19 crisis, massage services have also been suspended, affecting the lives of most people with disabilities, especially those with disabilities. Massage massage as well as other welfare work, as they used to rely on these occupations for a living.
I have a disability, my job is to be suspended, I can not do anything else, Mr. Men Sen Chamrong, representative of the Hand Massage Association, urged the Royal Government through the Ministry of Tourism to help prepare measures or standards for the health services business. This re-launched aims to make it possible for people with disabilities to have jobs again, just to solve their daily lives.
Mr. Men Sen Chamrong raised such practical proposals and challenges on the occasion of Cambodia participating in the celebration of the 41st World Tourism Day 2021 at the Ministry of Tourism on the morning of September 27.
"During the crisis of Kovid-19, which is spreading around the world, especially in Cambodia, we are making a serious impact on the disability sector, especially the blind who practice massage as well as the health sector," he said. "And so our business is often closed until now."
Mr. Chamrong continued that a large number of people with disabilities have lost their job opportunities completely due to the massage - massage business, etc. This is a job that directly affects the client. Despite being 100% vaccinated, customers do not dare to receive services from them.
He complained that due to all these problems, the family life of most people with disabilities has deteriorated significantly.
In response to Mr. Men Sen Chamrong's request, Minister Thong Khon ordered an expert officer to prepare an additional SOP standard procedure for this massage service. He said that a skill must have an SOP. This is to submit to the capital-provincial administration, as well as the competent authorities have specific standards to allow those businesses to reopen.
Mr. Thong Khon further advised that the re-opening of the massage service business must be a careful step to make the operation of the tourism business in the country run smoothly, safely and stably.
"The massage business is a closed location, contrary to the Ministry of Health's guidelines that do not allow people to go to a closed place, but we can move the massage parlor from the room that used to be closed," he said. "Any suitable place that can not be closed."
"Employees must be equipped with an alcohol thermometer and have a vaccination card for both masseurs and massage guests," he said.
He also encouraged each massage service business base to apply to the capital-provincial administration first, in which tourism officials should help inspect and organize properly for this business to operate and prevent Kovid's disease is contagious.
It is Mr. Chamrong's expectation that when the tourism sector is reopened and when more tourists visit Cambodia, it is hoped that the Royal Government, as well as the Ministry of Tourism and relevant ministries and institutions will help organize their business. He reopened well.
Mr. Sen Chamrong's last message was, "Without the participation of all of you, we as people with disabilities will be at higher risk of contracting this deadly disease or even losing our lives." . Indian Government Donates 1,500 Pump Wells to Tbong Khmum and Banteay Meanchey Provinces
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, chaired an online meeting (ZOOM) to hand over and receive 1,500 Aphrodite pump wells in Tbong Khmum and Banteay Meanchey provinces with a grant from Government of the Republic of India on the afternoon of September 27, 2021.
According to the report of Dr. Nuon Danhel, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Rural Development, the project to supply rural pump wells, which is managed and coordinated by the Ministry of Rural Development, has completed the construction of 1,190 pump wells in Tbong Khmum province, of which 689 in Memot district. Andong, Krouch Chhmar district received 46 wells and Tbong Khmum district received 455 wells.
In Banteay Meanchey province, there are 310 wells, of which Phnom Srok district received 46 wells, Serey Sophorn district received 100 wells, Preah Net Preah district received 43 wells, Mongkul Borei district received 26 wells, Malai district received 60 wells, Poipet city received 17 wells, Svay Chek district received 17 wells. Andong and Ochrov districts received 1 well.
Cambodia earns $ 326 million from rubber, rubber and timber exports in the first eight months of this year
In the first eight months of this year, Cambodia exported 194,525 tons of rubber, an increase of 14,605 tons, equivalent to 8% compared to the same period last year. The export of rubber wood reached 4,379 cubic meters.
The average selling price of rubber from the beginning of the year until August 2021 was $ 1,673 per ton, an increase of $ 386 per ton, equivalent to 30% compared to the same period last year.
Mr. Him Oun, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the General Department of Rubber, said that the current price of rubber is still profitable for farmers. For large companies, there are some difficulties due to high administrative costs, but not to the point of loss.
