(Phnom Penh): Addressing the United Nations General Assembly on the night of September 25, 2021, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, expressed his deep concern over a number of issues that threaten the peace, security and order of the United Nations. The world because of the challenge of the power of the world powers today.
In a video address attended by senior United Nations (UN) officials and leaders of several member countries, Samdech Techo Hun Sen said the world still has many challenges to overcome. Others include geopolitical rivalries and confrontations between superpowers, wars, technology and trade, armed conflicts, territorial disputes, terrorism and transnational crime, and climate change.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen further stated that Cambodia is a concrete example of the worst tragedy and, sadly, is now seeing the re-implementation of the same policy. If we can learn from the recent events that are happening in Afghanistan and many other events in the past, respecting the aspirations of each nation and the right of the people to make their own decisions is a great value. Huge.
For Samdech Techo Hun Sen to address the challenges as mentioned above, strengthening multilateralism and international cooperation based on the law is a necessary and priority.
For Samdech Techo Hun Sen, strengthening the policy of multilateralism and strengthening international solidarity is the United Nations as a core player and a beacon of hope. In this sense, Samdech Techo wants to see the United Nations strengthen its role to be more active and strong.
"I express my strong hope for the strengthening of multilateralism, with the United Nations at the heart of international solidarity, so that the international community can achieve victory over the Kovid-19 crisis," he said. And promoting peace, sustainable development and common prosperity for all of us. Multilateralism is our hope, and we look to the United Nations as our "light of hope."
He stressed that international peace and security are of paramount importance for a sustainable future. Cambodia reaffirms its long-held position that the primary responsibility for maintaining international peace and security rests with the United Nations.
He added that the changing realities of the 21st century require a United Nations that has a new form and is more responsive. It must adhere more firmly to a democratic, representative, and representative approach to problem solving.
Samdech Techo stated that Cambodia fully supports the United Nations Secretary-General's proactive efforts to make the organization more responsive to the needs of its member countries.
