(Preah Vihear): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment confirmed that so far the Ministry of Environment has been preparing to set up border posts in some wildlife sanctuaries. Well, make the protected area more secure from illegal encroachment by crooks.
"The demarcation of the border has made our natural resource protection area even safer, and we are now setting up checkpoints in areas such as the sanctuary," the spokesman said. Sre Pok forest, Phnom Prich wildlife sanctuary in Peam Krasop area.
At the same time, he stressed that the installation of the border post is necessary to identify each area and also necessary to prevent illegal encroachment on forest land, but the work of erecting the border post takes a long time. Because the process of setting up the post requires detailed inspection, the inspection of the border and the area of each protected area is large.
He said: "And now our professional officers are inspecting and setting up posts in some areas, such as the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary and in some wildlife sanctuaries and national parks. "We will push for clear demarcation work to prevent illegal encroachment and to strengthen the management of natural resource protection areas in accordance with the law on protected areas."
According to the senior official, the preparation of the state land register and the erection of posts are necessary to clarify how much land the state has to occupy by the Ministry of Environment.
In connection with the demarcation of the wildlife sanctuary, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the demarcation is very much needed for the participation of donor countries and partner organizations to participate in the organization of the demarcation. Post the border post After the state land is registered, the protected area will be much better.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, he also expressed confidence that the encroachment would decrease because all the natural resource conservation areas were legally declared as state lands, and now the officials know the location of the protected areas. However, some residents who encroached on state land to claim it did not know, citing the fact that no state border posts had been erected yet.
