For Cambodia, Deputy Prime Minister Yim Chhay Ly, Chairman of the Council for Agricultural and Rural Development, invited a representative of Samdech Techo Hun Sen to participate in this important meeting. The Deputy Prime Minister stated that it shows the vision as well as the commitment of the Royal Government of Cambodia to maintain food security despite the Kovid 19 crisis.
To ensure this food security, Deputy Prime Minister Yim Chhay Ly emphasized that the Royal Government of Cambodia has developed a roadmap on a food system for sustainable development in Cambodia by 2030. According to the Deputy Prime Minister, the roadmap is based on the results of dialogue and analysis and links existing policy frameworks and strategies.
In order to achieve the Vision of Food System 2030, which provides food security and nutrition for all, considering the sustainability of the economy, society and the environment, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set out four priority tasks, including:
First, Cambodia is committed by 2030 to make a healthy diet affordable and affordable for all Cambodians.
Second, Cambodia will continue to implement activities to promote gender equality, create decent work, develop entrepreneurs and provide employment opportunities for youth, women and vulnerable people, and provide a place for youth to participate in policy dialogue.
Third, in an environment where the whole world has been fighting Kovid 19 and climate change, Cambodia will continue to work to address vulnerabilities and reduce poverty, strengthen the resilience of stakeholders, and strengthen networks and infrastructure. Various.
Fourth, Cambodia will continue to organize dialogue, coordination and cooperation with all stakeholders to make the food system more environmentally responsive to the needs of vulnerable and indigenous consumers.
During the statement, Deputy Prime Minister Yim Chhay Ly emphasized at the meeting that Cambodia will chair ASEAN in 2022, an important time for Cambodia to strengthen cooperation with other countries to strengthen the food system. And sustainable for humanity, the planet and prosperity.
The Deputy Prime Minister also noted that as Cambodia becomes a high middle-income country by 2030, the food system is an important foundation for sustainable development, eradication of hunger and malnutrition, and cutting. Reduce poverty without leaving anyone behind.
