WASHINGTON, Sept. 21 (Xinhua) - In his address to the UN General Assembly for the first time during his term on Tuesday, President Joe Biden said the United States has no intention of provoking a "Cold War." No country wants to see the world divided or divided.
Such strict rhetoric and policy toward China has attracted domestic popularity and support, but will it be effective for foreign policy? Most experts believe that China did not pose the same threat as the former Soviet Union, and there are two reasons for this.
According to Chinese strategic experts, in the medium term, China just wants to become a superpower in East Asia to be on par with the United States and play a role in world order. But this does not mean that China may or does not want to have a world order ruled by China, but what China wants may be the integration of China's political system and values into other countries.
The United States and the West have always accused China of undermining democracy, but the reality is that democracy is not just an external threat, even in the United States, democracy has been killed behind pro-democracy groups. Former United States President Donald Trump stormed the National Assembly on January 6, 2021, at a time when major American newspapers ran headlines on the front page of "Democracy Day."
