The following is the announcement: Today, the 28th anniversary of the proclamation of the National Constitution, Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked his compatriots for participating in maintaining peace for the nation to prosper.
At the same time, the Prime Minister also explained the history of the historical establishment of the National Constitution in 1993.
In addition, the Head of the Royal Government thanked the compatriots who participated in maintaining peace for the nation to grow and prosper.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen posted the full message as follows: "Congratulations on the 28th anniversary of the proclamation of the National Constitution! September 24 is the 28th anniversary of the official promulgation of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Starting from the Paris Peace Agreement of October 23, 1991 on a joint solution to end the historic Cambodian crisis, which then on May 23-28, our country held its first elections under the auspices of United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNTAC). After the election, the national constitution was also made in history in 1993.
On September 24, 1993, in the Great Temple of Deva Vinich in the Royal Palace, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, the former King Father, signed the Royal Decree promulgating the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia. Official. September 24 is the birthday of the 1993 Constitution, which in 2021 is the 28-year-old Constitution of Cambodia.
The Constitution is the supreme law of the Kingdom of Cambodia. All laws and decisions of state institutions must be in strict accordance with the Constitution. Some of the most important points clearly stated in the Constitution are as follows: "Cambodia must strictly abide by the constitutional monarchy, liberal democracy and pluralism."
The Cambodian Constitution stipulates the principle of separation of powers: 1) the legislature, 2) the executive, and 3) the judiciary, and also enshrines the fundamental rights of citizens and the rule of law. Thank you, compatriots, for taking part in preserving the peace so that the country can prosper and prosper. Thank you for the peace. ”
