Private sector in tourism sector asks the government to announce the official opening date of international tourism to promote to international visitors and compete in the market first
(Phnom Penh): The Association of Private Agency Agencies operating in the tourism sector, both domestic and foreign, has urged the Royal Government of Cambodia to expedite the announcement of the "official reopening of international tourism time" to facilitate communication with external partners. Countries in promoting tourists, especially to attract international tourists before other countries.
Ms. Chhay Sivlin, President of CATA, said that the purpose of this press conference was to send a message to the Royal Government regarding the request to expedite the re-opening of international tourists, because the current situation is very important. For the restoration and promotion of national tourism.
She stressed that while regional and foreign countries are quiet now, Cambodia should be the first to seize the opportunity to receive international tourists.
Cambodia has a lot to offer tourists. In the past, it was not used to its full potential because before the international guests before visiting Cambodia, they visited the neighboring countries almost exhausted, so some goals in Cambodia they have not yet reached, but this new opportunity It is an opportunity for Cambodia to make the most of those tourism potentials. This is according to Ms. Chhay Sivlin.
"If the announcement is still delayed and the countries in the region open earlier, tourists will choose destinations outside of Cambodia, and travel agencies will rank Cambodia below the destinations of other countries," she said.
"Of course, the reopening of tourism will be a step-by-step process, but if we do not start now, the national tourism recovery situation will be much slower," he said.
In some countries, such as Costa Rica and the Dominican Republic, their tourism sector is almost as good as it was in 2019, while overseas partners are also waiting for answers in Cambodia, he said.
