Mr Rudd said the Australian government's unilateral decision to cancel the submarine deal with France and switch to nuclear-powered submarines was a "lightning strike". Do not put France as a partner, friend and ally.
The former Australian prime minister stressed that if the transition from conventional submarines to nuclear-powered submarines was for technical reasons, why did the Australian government refuse to talk to France directly? France also has nuclear-powered submarines.
On the other hand, according to Rudd, the nuclear submarines that Australia will receive under US assistance could become part of the US Navy because Australia does not have a civilian nuclear industry.
The United States, Britain and Australia recently announced a new security partnership called AUKUS, in which the United States and Britain will help Australia build nuclear-powered submarines. Therefore, Australia decided to cancel the agreement it signed in 2016 to buy 12 conventional submarines from France. France was outraged by the immediate withdrawal of its cabinets from the United States and Australia.
General knowledge: Why did Switzerland never take part in the war?
Switzerland has established the world's oldest military neutral policy. The country has not been involved in wars with foreigners since the principle of neutrality was established by the Treaty of Paris in 1815.
Although some European influences, such as Austria, France, the United Kingdom, Portugal, Prussia, Russia, Spain, and Sweden, agreed at the Vienna convention in May 1815 that Switzerland should remain neutral, the final ratification was postponed until the reign of the French emperor Napoleon. Failed, then, some coalition forces were able to invade France through Swiss territory.
The country has a history of military neutrality since the 16th century during the Reformation. Switzerland did not become a country at war since 1815 and did not join the United Nations until 2002. Switzerland has pursued an active foreign policy, and as a result, the country is often involved in the peace process around the world.
