Senior Minister Kun Kim: Do not let Kovid 19 cut off the search mission
(Oddar Meanchey): Senior Minister Kun Kim, Chairman of the Oddar Meanchey National Assistance Team, advised and encouraged students in the border area of Thailand to strive to overcome the difficulties caused by Kovid 19 to continue their life mission. Seek knowledge.
After being closed for several months due to the spread of Kovid 19, Hun Sen Anlong Veng High School on the morning of September 21, 2021, reopened to allow students to re-enroll. However, only 9th and 12th grade students are allowed to attend, as they are required to complete secondary school, high school and general education to enter higher education.
Oddar Meanchey is a province that is experiencing a high prevalence of 19 cases of Kovid and is also a province that receives workers from Thailand. This has made the province pay close attention to the fight against Kovid 19. The reopening of the school is also part of measures to prevent the spread of Kovid 19.
In each classroom, only half of the students are assigned to follow the distance control rules, and they are required to monitor their health, such as taking a temperature, before entering the classroom.
With the reopening of the school to allow students to return to school, Senior Minister Kun Kim also inspected the process of reopening the school and distributed alcohol and masks to teachers and students.
The senior minister reminded teachers and students to continue to adhere to the spirit of prevention of Kovid 19, especially the "3 do not and 3 prevent" rules.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister also mentioned the difficulties of the national society as well as the Cambodian family society due to the epidemic of Kovid 19 in the past two years. In particular, it has affected the education of students who are Cambodia's bamboo shoots.
Senior Minister Kun Kim also highlighted the efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to introduce measures to prevent Kovid 19 and solve national economic problems and people's livelihoods. In particular, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also paid close attention to the education of students who have missed school.
"We have missed school for a long time, which is why Samdech Techo decided to vaccinate children and young people from 6 to 18 years old. "The biggest intention of this decision is for our students to go to school and get a better education." This was confirmed by Senior Minister Kun Kim.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister encouraged all students to work hard to overcome the difficulties caused by Kovid 19. In particular, do not let this disease cut off education, which is a necessary factor for building a life and future.
National Wage Council will vote to set a figure for the proposed minimum wage in 2022
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training said that on September 28, 2021, the National Minimum Wage Council will hold a vote to determine a figure for the proposed minimum wage for workers in the textile sector. Garment and footwear production in 2022.
According to the announcement of the Ministry of Labor, the National Minimum Wage Council resumed its meeting on Tuesday, September 21, 2021 to discuss setting the minimum wage in the textile, garment and footwear sector for 2022. As a result, the three parties did not agree on a specific minimum wage figure.
The Ministry of Labor said that in order to achieve the 2022 minimum wage figure, representatives of the three parties proposed three different figures to submit to the National Minimum Wage Council for review and preparation for the final decision on September 28, 2021 according to the planned schedule.
Please be informed that in 2021, workers in the textile, garment and footwear sector will receive their minimum wage of 192 US dollars per month, despite the risk of spreading Kovid 19, the National Council for Money. The proposed minimum wage is $ 190, with another $ 2 added by the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
