BEIJING (Reuters) - Australia recently unilaterally rescinded a huge submarine trade agreement signed with France and resumed cooperation with the United States and Britain. And established a new security alliance.
This angered the French and also tarnished Australia's reputation by "betraying promises." But at a joint press conference on the announcement of a new security alliance hosted by the United States, Britain and Australia, during a video address, Biden forgot about Morrison, so Biden called him "one from Australia."
Australians do not understand why the US President said the name of the British Prime Minister so slowly but forgot the name of the Prime Minister of Australia? They are worried that Australia may be just another US chess player in the Asia-Pacific region. This means that Morrison's actions seem to be betting on Australia's fate and future.
First, a submarine trade agreement between the United States, Britain and Australia was signed on the basis of French-Australian betrayal. In addition to having to pay large sums of money to France, Australia also faced a crisis of French confidence to the point of retaliation.
Second, Australia's actions violate the spirit of the contract, whether in the diplomatic or economic spheres, will have a negative impact on Australia in the long run. Ask who wants to deepen cooperation with such a country?
Third, Australia's import of nuclear submarine technology, which has strategic military value, will cause the international community, especially its neighbors, to question whether Australia can honor its non-proliferation pledge.
Currently, key politicians such as the Prime Minister of Malaysia and the President of Indonesia have expressed concern that Australia's move could lead to a regional arms race. Now, under Morrison's political game, Australia is becoming more and more humble to the US satellite, which has become a joke.
