International Analysis: Will the US-France Diplomatic Dispute Make Europe Stop US Satellites?
PARIS (Reuters) - France on Monday canceled a meeting with British and Australian officials and is trying to persuade its allies in the European Union to support President McCormack's initiative to make Europe more autonomous. Stronger after the embarrassment of not being included in the AUKUS military alliance formed by the United States, Britain and Australia.
In 1999, the European Union agreed to create a force of between 50,000 and 60,000 troops, which could operate for 60 days, and in 2007, the bloc formed a network of military forces. With the participation of 1,500 troops from each member country. But these military forces could not take shape or operate, and experts believe that the failure was due to the incompetence of these military units.
Relying on France to form a European army is impossible, and combined with Britain leaving the EU, it makes this bloc lacking in planning, unable to defend itself. NATO without the United States is like a car without an engine, in other words, the United States remains the guarantor of security for Europe, whether Europe likes it or not.
Richard Whitman added that disagreements and criticisms of increasing European military budgets, as well as the perceived external threats, were also major obstacles to the idea of building an army. For example, in the eyes of the Baltic states, Russia is their biggest threat, but for Germany, Russia is a major gas partner and Hungary considers Russia an ally.
US President Joe Biden and French President Emmanuel Macron are reportedly expected to hold telephone talks in the coming days, according to the French side. Explain the AUKUS military alliance and the cancellation of the Australian submarine purchase agreement. But it remains to be seen whether Biden can ease France's anger.
