Under harsh weather conditions and combined with high tolerance, the sighting of this rare species was recorded at a research site where scientists collected feces and footprints during the dry season of the same year. These herds of mountain crocodiles are now living safely in their natural habitat under the strict protection of park rangers at the Srepok Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri province.
Bun Sothea, a member of the research team, described: "During this hatching season, our researchers have been monitoring regularly, and for this one-legged visit, we spent four nights to monitor the situation. Of mountain crocodiles at their various locations, from 7:00 pm until midnight, around 2:00 am.
"The excitement came after one of our team members spotted the newly hatched crocodile," he said with a smile.
The discovery of the current mountain crocodile herd is the first confirmed picture of the animal's survival after more than a decade of research in the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary, a protected area. Of the Eastern Landscape Forest is under the control of the Ministry of Environment and supported by WWF.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, said: “I would like to congratulate the research team and the law enforcement team for this great discovery. Without the determination and regular and consistent efforts of protected areas, we would not have achieved such great results.
We must further strengthen conservation and conservation efforts by prioritizing the search for appropriate and innovative solutions to support the management of protected areas and the protection of wildlife resources, and at the same time continue to call for To take better measures and methods to manage, use and manage natural resources.
The Ministry of Environment and WWF will continue to monitor the situation of mountain crocodiles in the Srepok area and strengthen measures to protect their habitats from threats such as land grabbing, encroachment and illegal activities. Others, by consistently implementing regular patrols by all means, both inside and outside protected areas.
