Samdech Techo Hun Sen: Some powers and allies continue to use all tricks to interfere in Cambodia's internal affairs (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, confirmed that some great powers and allies continue to use Every ploy threatens to interfere in Cambodia's internal affairs through the implementation of two standards of democracy and respect for human rights.
The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia stated: "Cambodia, we can not escape the effects of geopolitical conflicts between those great powers. "Some superpowers and allies continue to use all sorts of tricks to threaten to interfere in Cambodia's internal affairs through the implementation of two standards of democracy and respect for human rights."
Through the same message, Samdech Techo Hun Sen added that the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia have not forgotten the devastation and pain caused by the war that some great powers and their allies have put on us.
The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia added: "We also do not allow those countries to repay the moral debt they owe to the Cambodian people, but we just exercise our sovereignty as landowners, landowners to protect their survival. "The revitalization of the Cambodian people, peace, stability and national development only."
In this direction, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that Cambodia has firmly adhered to the policy of neutrality, non-alignment, peace, non-interference in internal affairs and mutual respect and mutual benefit. All countries in the world.
In addition, Samdech Techo Hun Sen said that Cambodia is very proud to have changed from a country that used to receive aid from the United Nations peacekeeping force to a country that is able to send a series of troops to participate in maintenance missions. Peace in countries experiencing the crisis of war.
From 2006 to 2021, Cambodia sent a total of 7,527 troops (including 468 women) to UN peacekeeping missions in 10 regions in nine countries, including Chad. Sudan Sudan - South, Central Africa, Lebanon, Cyprus, Syria, Mali and Yemen.
As of the end of June 2021, a total of 787 Cambodian forces (including 105 women) were on peacekeeping missions in South Sudan, Lebanon, Mali, Central Africa and Yemen.
On behalf of the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, I would like to express my deep gratitude and thanks to all the officers and soldiers of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces for their courageous sacrifice in the cause of national defense, sovereignty and territorial integrity. Both in the cause of peace in the world.
