Ros Sovacha, spokesman for the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, said that public and private educational institutions that have been allowed to re-operate must strictly adhere to the SOP principles in letter 50 and letter 46 of the Ministry of Education and guidelines. Issued on September 9, 2021 by the Phnom Penh Capital Administration on measures to prevent and control the spread of Kovid-19 to the reopening of public and private educational institutions.
Mr. Dim Virakrith, Principal of Chaktomuk Secondary School, stated that his school has complied with the main principles of the reopening of the school, which are required to be implemented, such as: set the class for a maximum of 15 students, wear masks to keep the gap clean. Spray regularly, measure heat, spray alcohol to keep food out and play together. He added that especially during school hours, there is a division of students who volunteer to study online and in the classroom.
According to the Phnom Penh Capital Administration, in the first phase, 227 schools were reopened, including 68 public schools and 159 private schools. Similar to public schools, private schools in Phnom Penh have also adhered to SOP principles in reopening classrooms.
Public and private schools have strongly supported the reopening of secondary and high schools. Those in the education sector expect that lower level classes may also reopen in the near future.
