China seeks application for accession to Trans-Pacific Free Trade Agreement (TPP) to expand economic influence in Asia
BEIJING (Reuters) - China on Friday actively pushed for applications to join the Trans-Pacific Free Trade Agreement (TPP), the largest free trade agreement in the Asia-Pacific region with 11 members. To expand its economy in the Asia-Pacific region. This is according to the Bangkok Post published on September 17, 2021.
China's accession to the agreement will have a significant impact on trade in the region. China's efforts are aimed at countering the intentions of the United States and other partners seeking to reduce its economic influence. However, it remains unclear whether China will be allowed to join the agreement.
Accession to the agreement requires the unanimous consent of all 11 member states, while China faces one obstacle: strained relations with Australia, one of the TPP members.
The Trans-Pacific Free Trade Agreement has 11 members: Japan, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, Brunei, Chile, Malaysia, Mexico, Peru, Singapore and Vietnam.
The Trans-Pacific Free Trade Agreement (TPP) was signed by 11 countries in Chile on March 8, 2018. The TPP was initiated by the US administration in the Obama administration, but the administration of President Donald Trump withdrew because it saw Donald Trump as "damaging to the United States and losing jobs". America.
It is estimated that if China joins the TPP, GDP will reach about 30% of global GDP, compared to the current 10%. In addition to China, Taiwan has also expressed interest in joining the agreement.
Japan, an economic leader in the TPP, has said compliance with high standards is a prerequisite for China to negotiate a free trade agreement.
