Aung San Suu Kyi to be tried by Myanmar court on further corruption charges
Former Myanmar leader Aung San Suu Kyi will be tried on corruption charges, which could land her in jail for decades. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday afternoon, September 17, 2021.
Suu Kyi has been under house arrest since she and her government were overthrown by the Myanmar military in a coup in February 2021. The 76-year-old Nobel Peace Prize laureate is currently on trial on charges of violating the COVID-19 ban, violating import laws and inciting social unrest.
A new trial on corruption charges against Suu Kyi will begin on October 1, 2021, her lawyer said Friday. Suu Kyi faces four corruption charges, each of which carries a maximum sentence of 15 years in prison.
The ongoing trial was postponed for two months as Myanmar suffered a severe outbreak of COVID-19 and only resumed this week, with Suu Kyi absent on the first day. Due to health problems.
