The following is the announcement of Takeo Provincial Administration: Three associations jointly promote financial literacy to young entrepreneurs and small and medium enterprises in Cambodia
The signing ceremony took place online (Zoom) on the afternoon of September 16, 2021, with the participation of more than 260 small and medium enterprises, banking and financial institutions and entrepreneurs, including: Live streaming via the official Facebook page of the three associations as well.
The Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) aims to strengthen and expand cooperation related to training, event organization and joint workshops to enhance financial literacy, fin-tech, environmentally friendly credit services and Build a network of young entrepreneurs in Cambodia and small and medium enterprises in Cambodia.
The signing ceremony of the Memorandum of Understanding was also accompanied by a special discussion forum on "Guide to Business Recovery in the Context of Kovid-19". The discussion focused on the methods and behaviors that banking and financial institutions have implemented to respond and support micro-enterprises, small and medium enterprises affected by Kovid 19.
Dr. In Channy, President of the Banking Association of Cambodia, said that through the Memorandum of Understanding, all parties will continue to strengthen financial literacy to the public, entrepreneurs and other small and medium enterprises.
Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia, said that the cooperation between the three associations is very important for the development of the microfinance sector in Cambodia to provide additional support to entrepreneurs in Cambodia, especially Young entrepreneurs by participating in promoting access to a wide range of responsible financial services.
"The cooperation of the three associations will be an important forum for financial professionals and young entrepreneurs to study, explore, develop and implement innovative solutions to drive development," he said. The business sector in Cambodia.
Mr. Seang Sopanha, President of the Cambodian Young Entrepreneurs Association, also highlighted that the difficulty of obtaining credit has always been raised by members of the Cambodian Young Entrepreneurs Association, and especially micro, small and medium enterprises in Cambodia due to the problem of Financing from financial institutions has hindered the growth of entrepreneurship in Cambodia.
He said that the problems that are difficult to get financing are the lack of collateral, high interest rates, lack of financial statements and lack of general information on the source of debt financing (Debt Financing). He added that equitable access to finance is the first priority of the 2025 strategy, in addition to the other five priorities of young Cambodian entrepreneurs.
"Based on our experience working with small and medium enterprises on access to finance, we have found that collateral is always a major factor in credit decisions," he said. Having adequate financial knowledge and documents is also important. ”
Mr. Sopanha continued, "This agreement is an important milestone in our partnership and sets out key strategies in the area of cooperation between the three associations on training, seminars, events and speakers. "Digital talk shows and access to finance among young Cambodian entrepreneurs."
