Thai government plans to cut 10 billion baht to prevent domestic rubber prices from falling sharply
(Bangkok): The Thai Rubber Policy Committee has approved in principle a rubber price guarantee scheme for 2021-2022, providing about 10 billion baht for annual projects operating for three consecutive years to ensure that Rubber prices in the market fall below the standard.
The project will be managed by the State Agricultural Bank and Agricultural Cooperatives, which will be implemented from October 2021 to September 2022. The ministry plans to submit the project to the cabinet for approval next month.
Thai Prime Minister Prayuth Chan-ocha has ordered stakeholders to monitor closely to ensure that there is no corruption and that the money reaches the real farmers.
As of September 15, the price of raw rubber was 48.40 baht per kilogram and liquid rubber was 48.50 baht per kilogram, according to the Thai Rubber Authority.
