Taliban guarantee security of Afghan money kept in banks
Kabul: Mohammad Idrees, acting governor of the Central Bank of Afghanistan, a Taliban government official, has come out to ensure the safety of Afghans' money kept in banks after they stormed it. Money because of worries. This was released by the Xinhua News Agency on Thursday, September 16, 2021.
"The Central Bank of Afghanistan reassures all Afghans that all banks in the country are under close control and are operating smoothly," Idris said in a statement. "All the banks are completely safe."
The statement comes as thousands of Afghans have been queuing up to withdraw their money from banks since the Taliban took control of the country in mid-August. Reports on the freezing of Afghan banking assets by the United States and the suspension of funding by the World Bank and the IMF have raised concerns among Afghans.
