Energy Minister Says Cambodia's Demand for Electricity Will Rise After Kovid 19 Declines
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Suy Sem, Minister of Mines and Energy, on September 16, 2021, confirmed that the demand for electricity in Cambodia will increase at a rate similar to the level before Kovid-19 or higher. More in the coming 2022.
Suy Sem made the remarks at the 15th East Asia Energy Ministerial Summit on "Cambodia's Energy Policy Policy, Challenges and Transitional Efforts Towards Carbon Reduction" this morning. This September 16, 2021.
"Although the global economy is expected to recover, we still face uncertainty about the spread of the Kovid 19 pandemic, especially its progression to the disease," he said. Other categories are faster than expected, which continues to disrupt the global economic recovery. Prior to the outbreak of the Kovid 19 epidemic, Cambodia's economy grew at an average rate of 7% per year, and electricity demand increased between 15% and 23%.
Minister Suem Sem added that in this context, the Royal Government of Cambodia recognizes that expanding access to modern, affordable and reliable electricity can at the same time reduce the density of greenhouse gas emissions. It is essential for Cambodia to achieve both the goals and the agenda of Cambodia's social and economic development and the energy transition goals towards carbon offsets.
At the same time, Suy Sem said that with the goal of reducing carbon emissions, he believes there is a need to accelerate the integration of digital technologies, clean fuels, renewable energy and clean energy technologies. And approval for use in each country's energy system.
He said that overall, Cambodia is committed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions in its energy production. To achieve this, we expect to generate energy from multiple renewable energy sources and energy efficiency projects in the near future. Indeed, it is an opportunity for the private sector and the government to work together to mobilize investment and financing to achieve this change.
