(Foreign): The recovery of the Taliban and the collapse of the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan is an incredible tragedy that has evoked psychological memories of past wars and policy discussions. There are already several references to the US withdrawal from Vietnam, an important historical comparison in assessing the impact of the situation in Afghanistan.
It is not difficult to compare images of the chaos in the air evacuation of Americans and Afghans with images of the United States leaving Southeast Asian countries. Many well-known media outlets have compared the situation with images of rooftops being evacuated by helicopter from Saigon, Vietnam.
No one can deny that the sudden departure of Americans from the roof of a hotel in Kabul looks like a picture of the chaotic evacuation of the US Embassy in Vietnam nearly 50 years ago. But those similarities really end there, and for Cambodia, for some reason, there may be more similarities than in Vietnam.
To His Excellency and Friends:
Prince Sisowath Sirik Matak was assassinated a week later.
However, this feeling of abandonment is not caused by the United States alone. Washington has led a campaign to overthrow the Taliban and attempt to rebuild the Republic of Afghanistan, but the United Nations and the international community have an equal share of responsibility for the situation in Afghanistan, as they have some responsibility for. What happened in Cambodia at the end of the 20th century as well.
One insight from the comparative situation between Cambodia and Afghanistan is that "nothing can replace American leadership." "American leadership remains a complex doubt, or at times necessary, in defense as well as in addressing most international security issues."
Another insight to gain an idea of the comparisons between Afghanistan and Cambodia is the breakthrough (or confirmation) of the pursuit of human development goals, such as the protection of human rights, the commitment to protect vulnerable populations. Disasters and support for civil society are qualified, conditional and transitional.
For skeptics or historians in particular, it is likely that this circumstance is almost non-existent, but there is no doubt that the international community's commitment to human development goals has been lost. Believe that this will be a problem for such goals in the future. The effects of this circumstance continue to evolve. The impact caused by the landscape in Afghanistan and Cambodia will continue to grow.
Despite the public promises of Taliban leaders, some circles remain skeptical that the pursuit of development goals in Afghanistan has been handed over to a terrorist organization. Such regimes have already shown a tendency toward violence and hostility against civil society, and the recent formation of their government is neither diverse nor comprehensive or consistent with what they are. Promised no reform.
The recent assumption that the Taliban may be different in the past is in stark contrast to what is actually happening. Beating innocent civilians in public, revenge on former government supporters, and restricting women from working and living free from oppression show that the worst is yet to come.
In other words, how many more atrocities are we willing to accept for politically necessary reasons? And to what extent will the recognition of a terrorist regime (or regime that creates terrorist activity) damage or destroy our credibility and integration as the world devotes everything to human rights and democracy?
Even though I saw those atrocities and used to taste lost, I did not feel like giving up. When I was young, I just knew that the United States was a land of hope, and that hope could save my life and the lives of others.
The Taliban's return to power and the collapse of Afghanistan should force an investigation into US foreign policy and the international community's commitment to human development. But it would be wrong to view the situation as an exclusive opportunity for the United States and the international community to influence Taliban-controlled Afghanistan.
Remarkably, Afghan youth have not been prominent on the media lately. The voices of Afghan youth are lost in analyzes from Afghanistan, in contrast to their overwhelming percentage of the population. It is estimated that almost 70 percent of Afghanistan's population is under the age of 25, but most of the voices heard come from former civil servants and adult refugees.
