MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin has told Syrian President Bashar al-Assad that the unauthorized presence of foreign armed forces in parts of Syria is a major problem facing the Middle East country. This is according to the release by the Xinhua news agency on Tuesday night, September 14, 2021.
Putin said during a meeting with Assad in Moscow on Monday that the presence of foreign forces, which was not approved by the United Nations or the Syrian leadership, was contrary to international law and hampered efforts to strengthen it. The country also slowed down the pace of post-war reconstruction.
At the same time, Putin hopes that Assad's actions in establishing dialogue with political rivals will continue, as only the unification of all forces in Syria will move the country forward.
