Australia is preparing more than $ 60 million in financing to help develop Cambodia's economic resilience
(Phnom Penh): In a meeting with senior Cambodian officials, Andreas Zurbrugg, Deputy Chief of Mission of the Australian Embassy in Cambodia, outlined the Australian Government's plan to provide A $ 90 million in financing, equivalent to more than US $ 60 million. Support the development of economic resilience in Cambodia for 5 years.
The confirmation of the grant plan was made during a courtesy call and discussion with Mr. Chheang Yanara, Deputy Prime Minister and Secretary-General of the Council for the Rehabilitation and Development of Cambodia of the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC) on 13 September. 2021. This is according to a CDC press release obtained by Fresh News on September 14.
The purpose of this meeting between Cambodia and Australia is to discuss the priorities of the bilateral development cooperation, the progress of the development plan to respond to Kovid-19 in Cambodia 2020-2022 and to inform the initiation of development programs for Cambodia in the future. The future.
Chheang Yanara praised the Australian Government's efforts to support Cambodia's socio-economic development, especially during the outbreak of Kovid-19.
He commended Australia for launching a program that is in line with the post-Kovid-19 strategic plan for socio-economic rehabilitation, focusing on recovery approaches, reforms and resilience.
Andreas also reaffirmed Australia's support for additional A $ 35 million in support of approximately 3 million doses of Kovid-19 vaccine through UNICEF and the World Health Organization to support the government's efforts in planning. Vaccinate people across the country.
The Minister thanked the Australian Government and people for their support for Cambodia's socio-economic development, especially Australia's commitment to providing safe and effective vaccines to Cambodians.
Six Asian countries under pressure to scrap new coal-fired power projects as climate summit approaches
KUALA LUMPUR: Six Asian countries are under pressure to scrap new coal-fired power projects as the climate change summit approaches. This was released by Reuters on Tuesday afternoon, September 14, 2021.
According to a new report released on Tuesday by E3G, new global coal-fired power generation projects have fallen 76 percent since the Paris Climate Agreement in 2015, with 44 countries agreeing. Closing new projects of their coal-fired power generation.
However, six countries in Asia, including China, India, Vietnam, Indonesia, Turkey and Bangladesh, remain coal-fired power producers, and if they agree, action could help. Cancel at least four-fifths of coal-fired power plants before construction begins.
