WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The World Bank on Monday released a report predicting that climate change could force up to 216 million people around the world to be displaced by 2050. This was released by the Xinhua News Agency on Tuesday, September 14, 2021.
Climate change is one of the strongest drivers of migration in the country due to its impact on people's livelihoods and the unfavorable living conditions of the affected areas, the report said. Strong.
According to the report, by 2050, sub-Saharan Africa could see 86 million internally displaced people. East Asia and the Pacific can see 49 million people. South Asia can see 40 million people. North Africa is visible to 19 million people. Allied America can see 17 million people. Eastern Europe and Central Asia can see 5 million people.
