International Analysis: 20 years after September 11, did the United States win the war on terror?
(Washington): Since the United States declared "war on terror around the world" in response to the terrorist attacks on New York and Washington on September 11, 2001, the mission and objectives of the effort This one has gone through repetition as the level and scope of the threat has changed over the decades.
Influential militants who have successfully attacked US territory, such as al-Qaeda, may have been neutralized, disintegrated, or even weakened. But terrorist groups supporting Al Qaeda ideology have spread to other parts of the world, including Africa, the Middle East and Asia.
There have been no signs of such a warning, and Taliban spokesman Zabihullah Mujahid has vowed that his government will not allow any group to use Afghan territory. Basically to attack other countries or neighboring countries of Afghanistan absolutely. But this is a promise, they still have to wait and see what happens.
However, in recent years, the American people themselves have begun to tolerate wars abroad, and even current US President Joe Biden has stated that the American era of war to help build other countries has reached. The end is over.
