(Phnom Penh): Climate change has made Cambodia more prone to floods and other disasters from year to year. The Royal Government of Cambodia, with the National Committee for Disaster Management as its staff, has paid close attention to the impact of these disasters, with the rescue operation being a special concern.
General Ith Sarath, Deputy Commander-in-Chief, Joint Chiefs of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Deputy Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, stated that in order to serve this disaster relief operation, the Royal Government as well as the National Committee for Disaster Management have decided to establish Search and Rescue ”in various units of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, such as the six regional armies, the two divisions, as well as some command.
General Ith Sarath confirmed that with the establishment of the "Search and Rescue Squad", the General Secretariat of the National Committee for Disaster Management has also opened training courses for all rescue forces, as well as equipped with some important rescue equipment. Also.
The 3rd Army Division also decided to establish a "Search and Rescue" force in August 2021, named "Search and Rescue Disaster Task Force No. 703" to prepare for the launch of natural disaster relief operations. Or other causes. The "Search and Rescue" force in the 3rd Army Division is also trained and equipped for some rescue operations.
General Ith Sarath emphasized that through the training and the provision of relief equipment, this is a demonstration of the spirit of the Royal Government of Cambodia, headed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, as well as all the forces to be ready. Ready for rescue operations.
General Ith Sarath also confirmed that the search force was always ready for the rescue operation, and not only natural disasters, but also catastrophic disasters were ready.
On the occasion of handing over the equipment to the rescue operation force in the 3rd Army Division, General Ith Sarath also reminded all search and rescue forces to continue training to increase the rescue capacity and to understand the disasters that occur in the country as well as in In the world.
General Ith Sarath also instructed the search and rescue forces to take care of the equipment provided to avoid damage or misuse during the operation. On that occasion, General Ith Sarath also stressed that all search and rescue forces must focus on rescuing the victims.
